# Annotated clean code

Every function, method, and class you write is type-annotated and readable on its own. Use Python 3.10+ syntax (`X | None`, `list[int]`). Use a concrete type; reach for `Any` only at a boundary that has no type.

## Annotations

- Parameters and a return type on every `def`, including `-> None`.
- Attribute types when the name does not already fix the type (`self.state: Tensor | None`).
- A one-line docstring on every public function and class stating what it is for. Skip a docstring that only repeats the signature.

```python
def settle(self, states: list[Tensor], y: Tensor, steps: int, lr: float) -> float:
    """Relax free states on local energy. Returns the learning rate of the last step."""
```

## Clean code

- A function does one thing. Split it when the name would need "and".
- Names say what the value is (`eval_lr`, not `tmp`).
- A comment records why, and only when the next reader would otherwise guess wrong.
- Leave the file without debug prints, unused names, or commented-out code.
- Match the surrounding module. A one-off stays inline.
